te.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-338094" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "linear"] ["text" "nonlinear"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-338094" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "nonlinear"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=(1+{x}^{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=1+{x}^{2},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "10f02d4e05e1514d523792d5d9e48497a88d0a14a3d5392cc77fed0b9f3315f7"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\,y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "10f02d4e05e1514d523792d5d9e48497a88d0a14a3d5392cc77fed0b9f3315f7"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=(x-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=x-1$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=2x.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $y,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=4,{f}_{2}(x)=0,{f}_{1}(x)=-{x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "10f02d4e05e1514d523792d5d9e48497a88d0a14a3d5392cc77fed0b9f3315f7"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{x}y$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)={e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the last term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "10f02d4e05e1514d523792d5d9e48497a88d0a14a3d5392cc77fed0b9f3315f7"} {"question" "q-337773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)={y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=-2,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is ${y}^{2},$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The last term contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{2},$ which fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "10f02d4e05e1514d523792d5d9e48497a88d0a14a3d5392cc77fed0b9f3315f7"} {"question" "q-337662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-337662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=2{cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})={sin}^{2}⁡x+4$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})+2L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x+2\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+4) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x+8 \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)+8 \\\\  & =2+8 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "10f02d4e05e1514d523792d5d9e48497a88d0a14a3d5392cc77fed0b9f3315f7"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-65035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=4x$ and $L({y}_{2})={x}^{2}+1$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-65035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})-2L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =4x-2({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}+4x-2 \\\\  & =-2({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-2(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-65035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "10f02d4e05e1514d523792d5d9e48497a88d0a14a3d5392cc77fed0b9f3315f7"} {"question" "q-65035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-65035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-(x-2)^{2}"] ["math" "-2(x-1)^{2}"] ["math" "-2{x}^{2}+4x"] ["math" "2(x+1)^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-65035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2(x-1)^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-79606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={x}^{2}+3x+5$ and $L({y}_{2})=x+2$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}-3{y}_{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-79606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}-3{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})-3L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5-3(x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5-3x-6 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-79606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "10f02d4e05e1514d523792d5d9e48497a88d0a14a3d5392cc77fed0b9f3315f7"} {"question" "q-79606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-79606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}+2x-1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+3"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+x+3"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-x-4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-79606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-130079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={cot}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})=3{csc}^{2}⁡x-4$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(4{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use ${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta =1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta .$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-130079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(4{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =4L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x-(3{csc}^{2}⁡x-4) \\\\  & =4{cot}^{2}⁡x+4-3{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =4(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)-3{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =4{csc}^{2}⁡x-3{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-130079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "10f02d4e05e1514d523792d5d9e48497a88d0a14a3d5392cc77fed0b9f3315f7"} {"question" "q-130079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-130079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2{csc}^{2}⁡x+4"] ["math" "3{csc}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "{csc}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "{csc}^{2}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-130079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{csc}^{2}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-130077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={e}^{x}+1$ and $L({y}_{2})=2{e}^{x}-3$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-130077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2({e}^{x}+1)-(2{e}^{x}-3) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+2-2{e}^{x}+3 \\\\  & =(2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{x})+(2+3) \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-130077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "10f02d4e05e1514d523792d5d9e48497a88d0a14a3d5392cc77fed0b9f3315f7"} {"question" "q-130077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-130077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{e}^{x}"] ["math" "4{e}^{x}"] ["math" "4{e}^{x}+5"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "{e}^{x}+5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "q-130077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1726288a19a32e552318d2a441375affcd1c6eabb6d29d8733236104e595980"} {"question" "e-6572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Show that the differential operator\n\n$$\nD(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} + \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nis nonlinear." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/example-6572.json" "file_sha256" "e2845bd713229cabf58c94e470f94622fe20a09fd120b89e9b1add26666030aa"} {"question" "e-6572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(ay_1 + by_2) = aL(y_1) + bL(y_2)\n$$\n\nfor functions $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =+{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & +a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & +ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(ay_1+by_2)  \\neq aD(y_1) + bD(y_2),$ the operator $D$ is nonlinear." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/example-6572.json" "file_sha256" "e2845bd713229cabf58c94e470f94622fe20a09fd120b89e9b1add26666030aa"} {"question" "e-21786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n\n- $D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y$\n\n- $D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/example-21786.json" "file_sha256" "299b21b964722794ec115c6619b884889e8a9b2c5288082a0200de3c7077c38a"} {"question" "e-21786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator $D_1$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\cos(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator $D_2$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_2(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}, f_1(x) = -\\sin(x),$ and $f_0(x) = 3.$\n\n- Operator $D_3$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_3(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3(x) = 1, f_2(x) = e^{x}, f_1(x) = 0,$ and $f_0(x) = -\\ln(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/example-21786.json" "file_sha256" "299b21b964722794ec115c6619b884889e8a9b2c5288082a0200de3c7077c38a"} {"question" "e-6569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_1) = \\tan^2{x}$ and $L(y_2) =2 \\sec^2{x}$ for some functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ What is $L(2y_1 -y_2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use $\\sec^2\\theta = 1+\\tan^2\\theta.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/example-6569.json" "file_sha256" "7c08e6aa710299e366c8e81d4f62701c266a8980a1519b8a413261eb81032a1a"} {"question" "e-6569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nL(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aL(y_1(x)) + bL(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identity $1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x,$ we can simplify this even further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nL(2y_1 -y_2)=-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072388/example-6569.json" "file_sha256" "7c08e6aa710299e366c8e81d4f62701c266a8980a1519b8a413261eb81032a1a"}] "usage" {"q-338104" [] "e-6569" [] "e-6572" [] "q-337662" [] "q-64900" [] "e-21786" [] "q-337739" [] "q-337765" [] "q-65035" [] "q-337728" [] "q-337774" []} "basis" 2717 "retractions" [[17592186229662 :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n1. $D_{1}(y) = x\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx}$\n2. $D_{2}(y) = y^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx}$\n3. $D_{3}(y) = 2\\frac{d^{3}y}{dx^{3}} - x\\frac{dy}{dx} + y^{2}$"] [17592186229697 :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_{1}) = 4x$ and $L(y_{2}) = x^{2} + 1$ for some functions $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$. What is $L(y_{1} - 2y_{2})$?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186228393 17592186228394 17592186228395 17592186228396 17592186228397 17592186228648 17592186228649 17592186228650 17592186228651 17592186228652 17592186340616 17592186340617 17592186340618 17592186340620 17592186340621 17592186340623 17592186340624 17592186340625 17592186340627 17592186340628 17592186340630 17592186340631 17592186340633 17592186340634 17592186340637 17592186340638 17592186340639 17592186340641 17592186340642 17592186340644 17592186340645 17592186340647 17592186340648 17592186340650 17592186340651 17592186340653 17592186340654 17592186340657 17592186340658 17592186340659